# Every parallel branch appends its `events.md` entry in the same place — EOF —
# and two branches adding different lines at EOF is the one merge git cannot
# resolve unaided. It fired the last time two surface slices landed in one wave
# (#174): the rebase raised a real conflict whose two sides were two complete
# entries sharing one trailing `___` rule, and a human had to read it out.
#
# `union` is git's built-in append-only resolution — it keeps BOTH sides' lines
# with no conflict markers. That is right for append/append and wrong for
# everything else: a NON-append edit to the tail entry — a correction, a
# redaction, a repaired separator — lands in the same hunk a concurrent append
# touches, and union settles it by keeping both sides, silently restoring the
# line the edit removed. Any merge carrying one has to be eyeballed; that it
# came back clean is not evidence that it is right.
#
# What union CANNOT do is keep two entries apart when they end with the same
# line. It emits a line both sides added exactly once, so while every entry
# closed with the same bare `___` rule, xdiff anchored each append before that
# shared rule and one rule came out for two entries — the next `time:` header
# then landed under the previous entry's prose and the two FUSED. #181 folded
# onto #180 that way. A rule now carries the identity of the entry it closes
# (`___ HH:MM <worktree>`), so no two appends share a line and there is nothing
# left to fold. AGENTS.md, "Repository Ledger", has the format and the three
# things it does not buy — this paragraph's non-append case among them; `node
# scripts/check-ledger.mjs events.md` is the guard on the bare rules that
# predate it, which an append-only ledger keeps forever.
#
# Deleting this file is caught by scripts/events-merge-driver.test.mjs, which
# also pins that property: a three-way parallel append keeps every entry's rule.
# CI's `guards` job runs it, so a delete goes red on the PR rather than waiting
# for someone to remember.
events.md merge=union
